ection could protect knowledge and resources potentially essential to ending future global famine, despite immediate starvation.","confidence":0.83},"academicOrientation":{"score":36,"confidence":0.88},"profileConfidence":0.93,"model":"gpt-5.6-luna","promptVersion":8,"inputHash":"1accde58f2ad6509a6235e845b10fd076f21aa2325c81969521b0c307d843fe3","reviewStatus":"unreviewed","validationWarnings":[]},"primarySubject":"Science","secondarySubjects":["History","War & Military"],"confidence":"high","rationale":"The book focuses primarily on the scientists' efforts and the significance of the seed bank during the Leningrad siege, framing it within the context of scientific discovery amid war and historical events.","method":"llm","model":"gpt-4o-mini","reviewStatus":"generated","inputHash":"d837153c7ab1d64565a6ad0713ca194a4f8d2553daaed0350df6a69b0b535474","summary":"'An astonishing story brilliantly told . . . It is as moving as it is gripping to read' Jonathan Dimbleby, author of Endgame: 1944 'A richly researched and meticulously observed account of a little-explored corner of 20th-century history' Guardian 'A fantastically well-researched history of science and sacrifice saturated in drama' i In the summer of 1941, German troops surrounded the Russian city of Leningrad - now St Petersburg - and began the longest blockade in recorded history. By the most conservative estimates, the siege would claim the lives of three-quarters of a million people. Most died of starvation. At the centre of the embattled city stood a converted palace that housed the greatest living plant library ever amassed - the world's first seed bank. After attempts to evacuate the collection failed, and as supplies dwindled, the scientists responsible faced a terrible decision: should they distribute the specimens to the starving population, or preserve them in the hope that they held the key to ending global famine? Drawing on previously unseen sources, The Forbidden Garden tells the remarkable and moving story of the botanists who remained at the Plant Institute during the darkest days of the siege, risking their lives in the name of science. 'A compelling account . . . a remarkable work of literary exhumation.","publicationYear":2025,"subjectCategories":[{"source":"google_books","scheme":"google_books_category","label":"History","sourceId":"source-google-books-summary-the-forbidden-garden-of-leningrad-a-true-story-of-science-and-sacrifice-in-a-city-under-siege-si"}],"displaySummary":"At the centre of the embattled city stood a converted palace that housed the greatest living plant library ever amassed - the world's first seed bank. After attempts to evacuate the collection failed, and as supplies dwindled, the scientists responsible faced a terrible decision: should they distribute the specimens to the starving population, or preserve them in the hope that they held the key to ending global famine? Drawing on previously unseen sources, The Forbidden Garden tells the remarkable and moving story of the botanists who remained at the Plant Institute during the darkest days of the siege, risking their lives in the name of science. 'A compelling account . . . a remarkable work of literary exhumation.","primaryTopic":"Science & Discovery","subjectEvidence":{"primarySubject":"Science","confidence":"medium","method":"evidence_score","candidates":[{"subject":"Science","score":8,"evidenceCount":1}],"evidence":[{"source":"llm_classifier","rawLabel":"Science","mappedSubject":"Science","score":8,"confidence":"high","note":"The book focuses primarily on the scientists' efforts and the significance of the seed bank during the Leningrad siege, framing it within the context of scientific discovery amid war and historical events.","id":"subject-evidence-book-the-forbidden-garden-of-leningrad-a-true-story-of-science-and-sacrifice-in-a-city-under-siege-si-1"},{"source":"google_books","scheme":"google_books_category","rawLabel":"History","mappedSubject":"History","score":3,"confiden